raph as its bipartite incidence graph (the Levi graph), which is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"how the graph algorithms reach it."}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/hypergraph","type":"topic","role":"symbol"},"doc://Grafluent/documentation/Grafluent/BipartiteGraphs-BipartiteGraph-Extensions":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Grafluent\/documentation\/Grafluent\/BipartiteGraphs-BipartiteGraph-Extensions","abstract":[{"text":"Members other modules add to ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/BipartiteGraphs\/documentation\/BipartiteGraphs\/BipartiteGraph","type":"reference"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"kind":"article","type":"topic","role":"collectionGroup","url":"\/documentation\/grafluent\/bipartitegraphs-bipartitegraph-extensions","title":"Extensions to BipartiteGraph"},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/BidirectionalDirectedGraph":{"fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"BidirectionalDirectedGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"BidirectionalDirectedGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/BidirectionalDirectedGraph","title":"BidirectionalDirectedGraph","abstract":[{"text":"A directed graph that also answers in-neighborhoods: Boost’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"BidirectionalGraph"},{"text":". Kept apart","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"from ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedGraph"},{"text":" because in-adjacency usually costs extra storage, and many algorithms only","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"go forward.","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/bidirectionaldirectedgraph","type":"topic","role":"symbol"},"doc://Grafluent/documentation/Grafluent":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Grafluent\/documentation\/Grafluent","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A generic, high-performance graph library for Swift."}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"collection","url":"\/documentation\/grafluent","title":"Grafluent"},"doc://Grafluent/documentation/Grafluent/DirectedAcyclicGraphModule-DirectedAcyclicGraph-Extensions":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Grafluent\/documentation\/Grafluent\/DirectedAcyclicGraphModule-DirectedAcyclicGraph-Extensions","title":"Extensions to DirectedAcyclicGraph","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Members other modules add to "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/DirectedAcyclicGraphModule\/documentation\/DirectedAcyclicGraphModule\/DirectedAcyclicGraph","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"article","url":"\/documentation\/grafluent\/directedacyclicgraphmodule-directedacyclicgraph-extensions","type":"topic","role":"collectionGroup"},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/DirectedHypergraph":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedHypergraph","abstract":[{"text":"A directed hypergraph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of hyperedges, each an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"ordered pair of vertex sets, its sources (tail) and its targets (head). Gallo, Longo, Pallottino","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and Nguyen, “Directed hypergraphs and applications”, ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Discrete Applied Mathematics"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"42(2–3):177–201, 1993, define a hyperarc as “an ordered pair … of (possibly empty) disjoint","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"subsets”; here either side may be empty and a vertex may be on both, the hypergraph form of a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"self-loop (XGI’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"DiHypergraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" allows both).","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"protocol"},{"kind":"text","text